I attended 
@le_book
 Connections event in LA yesterday and noticed a lot of things I hope the e-com community adopts: Programming Fewer panels (there was actually just 1) with more heavy hitters who actually know how to speak eloquently. Too many panels throw together some brands (to get them to come) with some sponsors (so they make $$$) without really caring about the quality of conversation and it ends up being super dry. Multiply that by 20 panels over 2 days and it's an nightmare. Half the seats are empty and of those who are sitting, very few are actually paying attention. The one panel at Connections had a successful consumer goods founder, SVP Brand @ Lululemon, and some creative agency founders who had done super cool work with the NFL, LeBron James, and more. 50-person room with 70 people crammed in listening intently (pic below - do you see a single person on their phone or laptop? no.) Sponsor <> Attendee Relationships I randomly went up to a bunch of sponsors (they each had a small table in the main conference room) and asked them about their experience conferencing. Crazy stat: all of them had sponsored previous events with Le Book. They were all returning. When you go to most e-comm conferences, a lot of sponsors are first-timers... which signals a ton of sponsor churn (and therefore little sponsor value). And here's why: I also went and chatted with 10+ individual attendees. I'm talking big dogs (CDs at companies like Skims) and asked them why they attended the conference. All of them said they wanted to meet potential creative vendors. THEY CAME TO MEET VENDORS. This is rarely the case in e-comm. You ask the brand-side folks at e-comm conferences why they're there and you get 1 of 3 responses: 1. They actually don't work FT at the brand and are there to find opportunities for their freelance/agency work 2. They actually don't work FT at the brand and are looking for clients for their SaaS 3. They're just there for a good time + to party (btw I am guilty of all three in the past but hey i don't make the rules) VERY FEW actually care about the content and vendors, and a very small % are true "brands" (outside of keynote panelists / speakers who literally come to speak and leave). *** Look, I love e-comm events. I love them because I see familiar faces and internet friends. I'll be the first to say that 
@CommerceRound
 and The Whalies are some of my favorites. 
@foundedbycelebs
 threw a great inaugural event last year. 
@iamshackelford
 crushes everything he does. But there's something we can all learn from how other orgs, in this case Le Book, host events.

