Today’s final “Honorable” mention is from Trainer win821 (
@trainer_novice
) in California, ! This postcard showcases a community mural Titled "Fortaleza" (meaning Fortitude or Strength in Spanish) painted directly onto the exterior facade of a Spanish-revival style Starbucks in Covina (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covina,_California…). The artwork acts as a visual tribute to community, collaboration, and everyday human labor. - The Figures: The piece illustrates elongated, abstract characters working in harmony, embodying a shared sense of resilience and support. - The Elements: It highlights physical, manual labor and connection—such as gathering materials, lifting baskets, and walking together. The flow of the figures is designed to echo a narrative of community growth and mutual aid. - Corporate Integration: Commissioned by Starbucks, the earthy tones and working motifs seamlessly tie together themes of global coffee harvesting with local community identity. The mural was painted by Liz Flores (https://lizfloresart.com), a self-taught, Chicago-based painter and muralist. Her signature style is defined by abstract figurative portraits, fluid shapes, and vibrant blocks of color. Her portfolio centers heavily around themes of womanhood, community, and Latinidad. She has partnered with several prominent brands, producing large-scale corporate artwork for companies like Target, Sephora, Lululemon, and the Chicago Bears. I have never seen a Starbucks with a Spanish tiled roof before! . That sticker match is funny!

